{"authored" [{"question" "q-51428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/a8fadcc272d96492194b2d46f1ae66151fb4fd16b2ee42a6840ff335aa0b4262.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-51428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed: \n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=3.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=5$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=5.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-51428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-51428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/5d776a61d1039639d91e2c667c68965d3e2c901cdc926e8c54e6356cf1d87c2e.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$ \n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-2$ and $x=2$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-6$ and $x=2$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-6,\\,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-6$ and $x=2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+6) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $\\,x=-1,\\,$ and $\\,x=1,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & ={e}^{-4}((-4)^{3}+3(-4)^{2})=-16{e}^{-4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2})=2{e}^{-1}>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}+3(1)^{2})=4e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is an inflection point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-16x+26) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-10x+10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=3,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-2(-1)^{2})=-3{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-2(1)^{2})=-e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{3}((3)^{3}-2(3)^{2})=9{e}^{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2,$ we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=2$ is an inflection point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "There are no points of inflection"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c2bf8a0e8598354823824dae50583b0644f0ae6afe93a13b3b022abdacc2df8" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3739.json" "file_sha256" "f818220a017ac07ad065640089031451ccb552b29aa585cbdec09cd4fad2ccf9" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3739.json" "file_sha256" "f818220a017ac07ad065640089031451ccb552b29aa585cbdec09cd4fad2ccf9" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3742.json" "file_sha256" "2d815d0e011baaaf3e8388700a4b2cdfeb16d116809efad81bdd769de5f52d70" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3742.json" "file_sha256" "2d815d0e011baaaf3e8388700a4b2cdfeb16d116809efad81bdd769de5f52d70" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3746.json" "file_sha256" "8ba4e7ba4649991a28c1656c41d794ccc95c97bcb65ffe479e193179cb80ecdf" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22\\\n \t\tf'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\n \t\tf''(x) & = 6x + 18\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6x+ 18 &= 0\\\n \t\t6x  &= -18\\\n \t\tx &= -3.\n}\n$$\n\nFinally,  we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3.$ So we pick two points around it, say,  $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4) & =6(-4)+18=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+18=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3746.json" "file_sha256" "8ba4e7ba4649991a28c1656c41d794ccc95c97bcb65ffe479e193179cb80ecdf" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3747.json" "file_sha256" "56849833c0042561d39745f6b1815fde431c655249cf9cc9a698d0a5d4badf2f" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-24(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-24(2)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3747.json" "file_sha256" "56849833c0042561d39745f6b1815fde431c655249cf9cc9a698d0a5d4badf2f" "basis" 1233 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-51253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/7d71b69f27d14ca6c9ff0467ecce1df7be2f53684b162a316dc8877218b202ca.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-51253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-2$ is not a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=-2,$   the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=3.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=0$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-51253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-51253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-cap