 two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=5.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-51428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-51428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-51428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=1$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=1$ and $x=5$"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"] ["text" "$x=3$ and $x=5$"] ["text" "$x=3$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-51428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=1$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/20b6532fc5d99c74a26bd752e19aa2a6451ea26a79d2a129e4c0203bc9133354.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-114402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_wid