 graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at  $x=-2,$  $x=0,$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-79199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-79199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-79199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=3"] ["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-79199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/826336bca267300c9b98e43072f74bb5efdacff0a4007fb7b938eb61893d8fbe.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has point of inflection at $x=2$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-114416" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114416" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\pm 1"] ["math" "x=\\pm 2"] ["text" "$f(x)$ has no inflection points"] ["text" "$x=-2$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114416" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=2$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $(0,2\\pi ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi \n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\pi .$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\pi ,$ we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-2753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "x=\\pi "] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-21047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=8{x}^{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-21047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =16x-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4}) & =16-\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}}=-112<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =16-\\frac{2}{(1)^{3}}=14>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-21047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-21047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-21047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-2"] ["math" "x=-4"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "x=4"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-21047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=0.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6(-1)=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =6(1)=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-2460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-6"] ["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=2"] ["math" "x=6"] ["text" "No inflection point exists"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-6x+7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+12x-6 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n12x+12=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=0$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)+12=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-2465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-1"] ["math" "x=-2"] ["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=1"] ["math" "x=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-2465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7)+{e}^{x}(2x-4) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3)+{e}^{x}(2x-2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)>0$ for all real values of $x,$ the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-114413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-1"] ["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=1"] ["math" "x=\\sqrt{2}"] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "There are no points of inflection"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)=3{x}^{5}+15{x}^{4}-2x+5.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}+60{x}^{3}-2 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 60{x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ and $x=1,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & =60(-4)^{3}+180(-4)^{2}=-960<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & =60(-1)^{3}+180(-1)^{2}=120>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & =60(1)^{3}+180(1)^{2}=240>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-344206" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344206" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-16x+26) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-10x+10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=3,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-2(-1)^{2})=-3{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-2(1)^{2})=-e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{3}((3)^{3}-2(3)^{2})=9{e}^{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2,$ we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=2$ is an inflection point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-2$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=2$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+6) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $\\,x=-1,\\,$ and $\\,x=1,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & ={e}^{-4}((-4)^{3}+3(-4)^{2})=-16{e}^{-4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2})=2{e}^{-1}>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}+3(1)^{2})=4e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is an inflection point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-20850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-2x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-20850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x-2 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(0) & =12(0-1)^{2}=12>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(2) & =12(2-1)^{2}=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-20850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ee3ddc13c2edc963e9bd9e50859c473c32a745668b82400b5d0a055d71c2994"} {"question" "q-20850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-20850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-1,1"] ["text" "$x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "q-20850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "There are no points of inflection"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b2eb7014625d312e0ec0bc0219751c7ec81df838e5dd38e6d70674cecd0bfe4"} {"question" "e-3739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/example-3739.json" "file_sha256" "7782d5e2e8cbd39945d245a145ad5922e2f00ddabc8051a70b5305bc43d686a4"} {"question" "e-3739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/example-3739.json" "file_sha256" "7782d5e2e8cbd39945d245a145ad5922e2f00ddabc8051a70b5305bc43d686a4"} {"question" "e-3742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/example-3742.json" "file_sha256" "2e85a8b003e781d07be10ac18c8744013f0c5a74eed7bceb9ab635d8715cf538"} {"question" "e-3742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/example-3742.json" "file_sha256" "2e85a8b003e781d07be10ac18c8744013f0c5a74eed7bceb9ab635d8715cf538"} {"question" "e-3746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/example-3746.json" "file_sha256" "8ba4e7ba4649991a28c1656c41d794ccc95c97bcb65ffe479e193179cb80ecdf"} {"question" "e-3746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22\\\n \t\tf'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\n \t\tf''(x) & = 6x + 18\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6x+ 18 &= 0\\\n \t\t6x  &= -18\\\n \t\tx &= -3.\n}\n$$\n\nFinally,  we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3.$ So we pick two points around it, say,  $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4) & =6(-4)+18=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+18=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/example-3746.json" "file_sha256" "8ba4e7ba4649991a28c1656c41d794ccc95c97bcb65ffe479e193179cb80ecdf"} {"question" "e-3747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/example-3747.json" "file_sha256" "56849833c0042561d39745f6b1815fde431c655249cf9cc9a698d0a5d4badf2f"} {"question" "e-3747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-24(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-24(2)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/example-3747.json" "file_sha256" "56849833c0042561d39745f6b1815fde431c655249cf9cc9a698d0a5d4badf2f"}] "usage" {"e-3746" [] "q-20850" [] "q-51428" [] "q-31219" [] "q-344246" [] "q-344063" [] "q-114407" [] "e-3742" [] "q-31213" [] "e-3747" [] "e-3739" []} "basis" 1302 "retractions" [[17592186097238 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/a8fadcc272d96492194b2d46f1ae66151fb4fd16b2ee42a6840ff335aa0b4262.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?"] [17592186097137 :q